WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:07.140
Mindy Grossman has spent
her professional life

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:10.433
thinking about what we want to
buy and how we want to feel.

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:13.800
From fashion with Tommy
Hilfiger and Ralph Lauren,

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:15.290
to Nike with Phil Knight,

00:00:15.290 --> 00:00:17.720
and the Home Shopping
Network with Barry Diller,

00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:20.210
she's worked alongside
some of the most innovative

00:00:20.210 --> 00:00:23.170
and iconoclastic
entrepreneurs of our time,

00:00:23.170 --> 00:00:26.610
constantly pushing herself just
beyond what was comfortable.

00:00:26.610 --> 00:00:30.340
I don't believe I've truly been 100% ready

00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:33.570
for any job I've ever taken.

00:00:33.570 --> 00:00:36.070
Now she's got arguably her biggest job

00:00:36.070 --> 00:00:38.760
and the biggest challenge of her career.

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:41.850
WeightWatchers, which
Grossman has rebranded WW,

00:00:41.850 --> 00:00:43.200
is more than a company.

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:45.310
It's a way of life, a state of mind

00:00:45.310 --> 00:00:47.670
and maybe exactly the
sort of tricky, messy,

00:00:47.670 --> 00:00:51.930
human-focused endeavor she's
been preparing for all along.

00:00:51.930 --> 00:00:53.400
Add in to those challenges,

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:55.670
in the past year, she's
faced a global pandemic

00:00:55.670 --> 00:00:59.160
and some harsh criticism
over some of her decisions.

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:00.400
I spoke with Mindy to find out

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:02.170
what a career squarely focused

00:01:02.170 --> 00:01:05.010
on bringing your true
feelings and experiences

00:01:05.010 --> 00:01:07.960
can tell us about the changing
role of the modern leader.

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:10.610
I think too many women have felt

00:01:10.610 --> 00:01:12.780
that they don't put themselves out there

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:15.003
because they have to wait to be perfect.

00:01:24.980 --> 00:01:27.970
First of all, I'm so grateful
to you for doing this

00:01:27.970 --> 00:01:32.220
and opening up your home virtually to me.

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:34.870
Originally you set out to be a lawyer,

00:01:34.870 --> 00:01:37.450
or that's what you thought
you were gonna do, true?

00:01:37.450 --> 00:01:38.380
Absolutely.

00:01:38.380 --> 00:01:40.690
From the time I was a little girl

00:01:40.690 --> 00:01:43.443
watching Perry Mason with my grandmother,

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:47.320
I was convinced that I
wanted to be a lawyer,

00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:49.483
and actually ultimately a judge.

00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:52.860
I'm an adopted child.

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:55.960
And when I was about four or five,

00:01:55.960 --> 00:01:59.930
I had to go into the court
and there was a judge.

00:01:59.930 --> 00:02:02.380
It actually happened to be a female judge,

00:02:02.380 --> 00:02:04.177
which was unusual at that time.

00:02:04.177 --> 00:02:06.490
And I remember going up to her

00:02:06.490 --> 00:02:11.490
and she affirmed that I was
gonna be with my parents.

00:02:11.690 --> 00:02:14.930
And I said, I wanna be
someone that can have

00:02:14.930 --> 00:02:18.300
that kind of impact on people.

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:20.810
And that was gonna be my approach.

00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:24.800
And I was an English literature
and philosophy major,

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:29.800
planning to go to law
school in the fall of '78.

00:02:29.830 --> 00:02:33.610
But I made the pretty radical decision.

00:02:33.610 --> 00:02:36.597
And remember calling
my parents and I said,

00:02:36.597 --> 00:02:38.837
"I've decided not to go to law school

00:02:38.837 --> 00:02:41.760
"and I'm leaving right now
and I'm moving to New York."

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:45.560
And I think that moment was defining

00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:49.470
because I realized for a fact

00:02:49.470 --> 00:02:52.580
that not having taken that risk

00:02:52.580 --> 00:02:55.530
would actually have been riskier

00:02:55.530 --> 00:02:59.660
than taking the risk
and redefining my path.

00:02:59.660 --> 00:03:03.090
And so you end up not a lawyer.

00:03:03.090 --> 00:03:05.870
You end up in the menswear
business for quite some time.

00:03:05.870 --> 00:03:08.780
Like, that's where you start out.

00:03:08.780 --> 00:03:12.670
Was that purposeful or
was that just the job

00:03:12.670 --> 00:03:13.503
that was available?

00:03:13.503 --> 00:03:18.460
Because you make your way
through that leg of the business

00:03:18.460 --> 00:03:21.000
pretty dramatically and pretty quickly.

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:23.410
Yeah, when I first moved to New York,

00:03:23.410 --> 00:03:25.970
I had an interview at a big

00:03:25.970 --> 00:03:30.220
multi-brand menswear conglomerate.

00:03:30.220 --> 00:03:33.570
Actually, at the time, there
weren't very many women

00:03:33.570 --> 00:03:35.330
in the menswear business.

00:03:35.330 --> 00:03:38.500
And I went to work for the president

00:03:38.500 --> 00:03:40.370
of the international division

00:03:40.370 --> 00:03:44.890
of the menswear side of the company.

00:03:44.890 --> 00:03:48.230
And that really set me up on the course

00:03:48.230 --> 00:03:50.540
of then staying in the menswear industry

00:03:50.540 --> 00:03:52.483
for over 15 years.

00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:57.540
I worked for Ron Chereskin, Jeffrey Banks,

00:03:57.540 --> 00:03:59.440
then Merona Sport.

00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:01.730
Then I had the opportunity
to work for Willi Smith,

00:04:01.730 --> 00:04:05.150
who was an incredible designer in the 80s

00:04:05.150 --> 00:04:10.150
who really believed in culture
driving fashion and change.

00:04:11.420 --> 00:04:15.210
Then worked for Tommy Hilfiger
and then Ralph Lauren.

00:04:15.210 --> 00:04:18.030
So how does your style develop

00:04:18.030 --> 00:04:20.280
working for that series of people?

00:04:20.280 --> 00:04:22.050
So I think there's a couple of things

00:04:22.050 --> 00:04:25.400
that have defined my leadership style.

00:04:25.400 --> 00:04:29.410
One is I pretty much have wholly

00:04:29.410 --> 00:04:33.480
worked for entrepreneurs my entire career.

00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:35.880
Right.
So I've always had

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:40.880
this intrapreneur curiosity,
passion around what it is I do.

00:04:45.350 --> 00:04:46.700
I'd say the second thing

00:04:46.700 --> 00:04:49.580
that defined a lot of my leadership style

00:04:49.580 --> 00:04:53.010
is I never believed that
I needed to bifurcate

00:04:53.010 --> 00:04:57.350
my work life and my personal life.

00:04:57.350 --> 00:04:59.570
It's hard enough being one Mindy,

00:04:59.570 --> 00:05:01.890
nevertheless trying to be two.

00:05:01.890 --> 00:05:05.400
And that was a little
difficult in starting out,

00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:08.240
even with a family and being a mom

00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:10.520
and being a wife and working,

00:05:10.520 --> 00:05:15.230
but I always felt it
made me more accessible

00:05:15.230 --> 00:05:17.330
and have more humanity.

00:05:17.330 --> 00:05:21.030
And if you look at the pandemic
and what we've been through,

00:05:21.030 --> 00:05:24.390
people literally look up to leaders

00:05:24.390 --> 00:05:29.390
who are willing to show
themselves and show empathy

00:05:30.410 --> 00:05:35.410
and really show care across
all areas of the business,

00:05:36.140 --> 00:05:40.393
financial, social, and human.

00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:42.840
So one of my favorite things about you,

00:05:42.840 --> 00:05:46.080
and full disclosure,
you and I are friends.

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:49.060
I've become friendly with both
you and your husband, Neil.

00:05:49.060 --> 00:05:51.140
And one of the fun things that I got to do

00:05:51.140 --> 00:05:52.710
is talk to Neil a little bit about you.

00:05:52.710 --> 00:05:56.360
So I'm gonna have you
watch something, if we can,

00:05:56.360 --> 00:05:58.980
that he told me when I talked to him

00:05:58.980 --> 00:06:01.360
and I asked him just to tell a story

00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:03.070
that he felt captured your relationship.

00:06:03.070 --> 00:06:03.903
You guys have been married

00:06:03.903 --> 00:06:06.310
for 33 years.
Should I be nervous?

00:06:06.310 --> 00:06:09.070
No, I think you're gonna,

00:06:09.070 --> 00:06:10.670
get a kick out of this.
Okay.

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:15.120
We have a phenomenal daughter.

00:06:15.120 --> 00:06:17.130
You've met her, I believe.

00:06:17.130 --> 00:06:22.130
But she was your
typical-ish 14, 15-year-old.

00:06:22.810 --> 00:06:27.260
And she was having
friends for a sleep over

00:06:27.260 --> 00:06:29.990
and they were gonna go
party, New York City kids.

00:06:29.990 --> 00:06:33.370
And they go out and they're
all dressed to the nines.

00:06:33.370 --> 00:06:35.590
One of them comes home
at about 8:30 and says,

00:06:35.590 --> 00:06:36.930
I'm a little tired, goes to sleep.

00:06:36.930 --> 00:06:39.193
And the rest of them show
up about an hour later.

00:06:39.193 --> 00:06:41.743
And it was sort of one of those moments.

00:06:42.880 --> 00:06:46.660
And I looked at my wife
and said, "Can I help?"

00:06:46.660 --> 00:06:48.500
And her answer was no.

00:06:48.500 --> 00:06:49.810
That was probably 9:30.

00:06:49.810 --> 00:06:53.370
And she's finished dealing with the issue

00:06:53.370 --> 00:06:55.323
at about 12:30 or 1.

00:06:56.680 --> 00:07:01.050
And in she comes and
she's sort of steaming,

00:07:01.050 --> 00:07:03.070
not so much with them, but me.

00:07:03.070 --> 00:07:06.597
And she says, "Some help you are."

00:07:06.597 --> 00:07:09.147
And I said, "I don't work for you."

00:07:11.170 --> 00:07:12.927
And she looked back and
said, "That's right.

00:07:12.927 --> 00:07:15.430
"You would have been
fired a long time ago."

00:07:16.382 --> 00:07:20.243
I so remember that moment.

00:07:21.170 --> 00:07:22.590
You remember that?

00:07:22.590 --> 00:07:24.810
I so remember that.

00:07:24.810 --> 00:07:29.560
And the funny part is we are
such opposite human beings.

00:07:29.560 --> 00:07:33.860
Neil is a mathematical
physicist at his core

00:07:33.860 --> 00:07:36.400
and some of that is black and white.

00:07:36.400 --> 00:07:41.040
And, you know, I'm kind of
opposite on the spectrum.

00:07:41.040 --> 00:07:45.410
So some of the funniest
times in our marriage

00:07:45.410 --> 00:07:49.600
has kind of been laughing
at the differences.

00:07:49.600 --> 00:07:52.860
And the one thing he didn't mention,

00:07:52.860 --> 00:07:57.087
the second I said, "Yes,
you don't work for me

00:07:57.087 --> 00:07:58.870
"because I would fire you,"

00:07:58.870 --> 00:08:01.480
we got hysterical laughing.
Yeah.

00:08:01.480 --> 00:08:04.840
And there has to be
those moments of tension

00:08:04.840 --> 00:08:07.970
that can turn into humor.

00:08:07.970 --> 00:08:09.853
That's what life is, right?

00:08:14.060 --> 00:08:15.720
Like so many high-powered executives,

00:08:15.720 --> 00:08:18.200
the pandemic has kept Mindy in one place,

00:08:18.200 --> 00:08:21.100
her home in the Hudson Valley,
north of New York City.

00:08:21.100 --> 00:08:24.090
It's a living testament to
her and Neil's eclectic taste,

00:08:24.090 --> 00:08:25.540
along with a sense of whimsy.

00:08:26.450 --> 00:08:27.893
She gave me a virtual tour.

00:08:28.820 --> 00:08:32.100
So every room has its own
different personality.

00:08:32.100 --> 00:08:34.870
Ah, there you see him in the sunroom.

00:08:34.870 --> 00:08:36.470
Oh, there he is, hard at work.

00:08:38.840 --> 00:08:43.840
One of my favorites is this room.

00:08:44.120 --> 00:08:48.170
And this is part library.
part dining room,

00:08:48.170 --> 00:08:50.650
part art installation.

00:08:50.650 --> 00:08:55.650
If I go around, there's
Marilyn, Salvador Dali,

00:08:55.990 --> 00:08:59.910
Marie Antoinette, Mick Jagger,

00:08:59.910 --> 00:09:03.560
Einstein, Frida Kahlo,

00:09:03.560 --> 00:09:07.663
Audrey Hepburn, and Abraham Lincoln.

00:09:08.890 --> 00:09:13.890
This is where I've been
working since March.

00:09:13.910 --> 00:09:16.280
And you can see the eclectic nature

00:09:16.280 --> 00:09:18.773
of our reading and our books.

00:09:20.490 --> 00:09:22.523
This is actually Robert the Bruce.

00:09:23.420 --> 00:09:25.973
And it actually opens.

00:09:28.500 --> 00:09:31.570
And this is Tartan House.

00:09:31.570 --> 00:09:34.280
It's been kind of a labor of love

00:09:34.280 --> 00:09:38.770
that we've been up here, which
to a degree, it's a gift.

00:09:38.770 --> 00:09:41.040
I never would have spent
as much time up here.

00:09:41.040 --> 00:09:43.393
So it's been a lot of fun with the family.

00:09:47.790 --> 00:09:49.830
All the while, Grossman
has been continuing

00:09:49.830 --> 00:09:53.833
a corporate transformation at
WW one Zoom call at a time.

00:09:54.710 --> 00:09:57.700
The turnaround hasn't been
easy or universally hailed,

00:09:57.700 --> 00:10:01.040
especially by shareholders
who were wildly enthusiastic

00:10:01.040 --> 00:10:02.023
and then skeptical.

00:10:02.913 --> 00:10:06.657
[Man On TV Commercial] Hey,
Sally, you still on that diet?

00:10:06.657 --> 00:10:09.090
Grossman and her team
had the delicate task

00:10:09.090 --> 00:10:12.420
of radically reshaping
a brand long associated

00:10:12.420 --> 00:10:14.236
simply with dropping pounds.

00:10:14.236 --> 00:10:15.230
So I got through the party

00:10:15.230 --> 00:10:17.340
without eating one bite of cake.

00:10:17.340 --> 00:10:19.810
To an era that has a much
more holistic approach

00:10:19.810 --> 00:10:22.352
to living one's best life.

00:10:22.352 --> 00:10:24.530
When the company hired Grossman in 2018,

00:10:24.530 --> 00:10:28.160
the brand felt antiquated
and candidly out of touch.

00:10:28.160 --> 00:10:30.930
Clearly WeightWatchers was a brand

00:10:30.930 --> 00:10:33.494
that had been around forever.

00:10:33.494 --> 00:10:35.720
But if we were gonna go into the future,

00:10:35.720 --> 00:10:39.030
we couldn't just be defined by weight.

00:10:39.030 --> 00:10:40.850
I know people laughed at me a little

00:10:40.850 --> 00:10:43.230
when I said we are gonna ban the use

00:10:43.230 --> 00:10:46.280
of before and after pictures forever

00:10:46.280 --> 00:10:49.270
because health is a journey.

00:10:49.270 --> 00:10:52.530
And what I want at this point in my life,

00:10:52.530 --> 00:10:56.150
and what I need is very
different than what my daughter,

00:10:56.150 --> 00:10:57.950
who's a young mom needs.

00:10:57.950 --> 00:11:01.510
And today we don't tell
you what you have to weigh.

00:11:01.510 --> 00:11:04.650
What we say is, what
does healthy mean to you?

00:11:04.650 --> 00:11:08.320
What are your goals and how
do we help you get there?

00:11:08.320 --> 00:11:11.773
And that's not about a number on a scale.

00:11:12.820 --> 00:11:16.260
Among that sort of reassessment,

00:11:16.260 --> 00:11:18.950
how do we think about our wellness?

00:11:18.950 --> 00:11:23.170
Because that has all changed as well.

00:11:23.170 --> 00:11:25.207
And I wonder how you think about it.

00:11:25.207 --> 00:11:26.540
You know, it's interesting.

00:11:26.540 --> 00:11:31.130
When I joined the company a
little over three years ago,

00:11:31.130 --> 00:11:33.510
we were talking about wellness

00:11:33.510 --> 00:11:35.640
and talking about the wellness economy

00:11:35.640 --> 00:11:37.610
and the trillions of dollars,

00:11:37.610 --> 00:11:40.460
but no one in the world
was getting healthier.

00:11:40.460 --> 00:11:45.030
And this pandemic has only
heightened that reality.

00:11:45.030 --> 00:11:49.340
And wellness has absolutely gone

00:11:49.340 --> 00:11:52.480
from being a luxury to a necessity,

00:11:52.480 --> 00:11:57.010
especially if you look at COVID
and the leading indicators

00:11:57.010 --> 00:12:00.400
and cause of death around
obesity and diabetes.

00:12:00.400 --> 00:12:04.270
I'd say the second thing
that is critical today,

00:12:04.270 --> 00:12:09.270
the trust factor around
brands has really heightened.

00:12:10.060 --> 00:12:13.330
There's so much misinformation out there.

00:12:13.330 --> 00:12:15.830
And if you can be that trusted brand

00:12:15.830 --> 00:12:18.090
and that partner for people,

00:12:18.090 --> 00:12:22.110
that is a significant opportunity.

00:12:22.110 --> 00:12:24.790
And then lastly, if you could build it all

00:12:24.790 --> 00:12:27.200
around the power of community

00:12:27.200 --> 00:12:31.480
and bring people together
around common values,

00:12:31.480 --> 00:12:33.563
today, that's more important than ever.

00:12:35.940 --> 00:12:36.773
Grossman's tenure

00:12:36.773 --> 00:12:38.670
has not been without controversy.

00:12:38.670 --> 00:12:40.530
After firing thousands of workers

00:12:40.530 --> 00:12:42.820
over Zoom calls during the pandemic,

00:12:42.820 --> 00:12:45.650
she and her team faced a
raft of public criticism.

00:12:45.650 --> 00:12:47.690
They literally just said,

00:12:47.690 --> 00:12:49.543
I'm gonna be reading from a script.

00:12:50.630 --> 00:12:52.470
You will not be able to speak.

00:12:52.470 --> 00:12:54.090
The women say it was all over

00:12:54.090 --> 00:12:55.970
after just three minutes.

00:12:55.970 --> 00:12:58.490
I had never been fired before.

00:12:58.490 --> 00:13:01.500
So here I am, 66 years old.

00:13:01.500 --> 00:13:02.850
Grossman defended the decision

00:13:02.850 --> 00:13:06.220
as part of the company's
inevitable digital slimming down.

00:13:06.220 --> 00:13:10.970
We have made the decision
to accelerate our efforts,

00:13:10.970 --> 00:13:13.280
particularly in digital and virtual.

00:13:13.280 --> 00:13:16.560
And unfortunately that had us make

00:13:16.560 --> 00:13:18.940
the very difficult decision

00:13:18.940 --> 00:13:23.330
that affected a number of
employees around the world.

00:13:23.330 --> 00:13:26.430
This past year was brutal
for so many businesses.

00:13:26.430 --> 00:13:28.270
You had to lay people off.

00:13:28.270 --> 00:13:31.190
You faced criticism for
the way that you did that.

00:13:31.190 --> 00:13:33.710
Like, how does that feel in the moment

00:13:33.710 --> 00:13:36.530
and how do you sort of
keep that North Star?

00:13:36.530 --> 00:13:41.360
Look, no one enjoys, right,

00:13:41.360 --> 00:13:44.900
when people are coming at you
for something you've done.

00:13:44.900 --> 00:13:48.800
But the way I think
about it is any decision

00:13:48.800 --> 00:13:51.610
that I've ever had to make

00:13:51.610 --> 00:13:54.960
that affects anyone in any way

00:13:54.960 --> 00:13:59.930
is a very carefully humane decision.

00:13:59.930 --> 00:14:03.370
Now we came into the year
with the greatest momentum

00:14:03.370 --> 00:14:07.560
the company's ever had,
and then the world shifted.

00:14:07.560 --> 00:14:12.300
And we had to make the
decision to close our offices

00:14:12.300 --> 00:14:16.890
and pause every one of
our physical workshops.

00:14:16.890 --> 00:14:21.190
But we trained 14,000 coaches and guides

00:14:21.190 --> 00:14:25.990
and simultaneously launched
all those workshops virtually

00:14:25.990 --> 00:14:28.680
in 12 different markets.

00:14:28.680 --> 00:14:31.900
So the learnings from
that have now created

00:14:31.900 --> 00:14:34.453
a whole new opportunity for us.

00:14:38.520 --> 00:14:40.820
Grossman had a not-so-secret
weapon to deploy

00:14:40.820 --> 00:14:43.303
when it came to WW, Oprah.

00:14:45.350 --> 00:14:47.300
The woman who taught the world how to feel

00:14:47.300 --> 00:14:49.580
and has publicly struggled
with her own weight

00:14:49.580 --> 00:14:51.950
took a stake in WeightWatchers in 2015

00:14:51.950 --> 00:14:54.580
and became an ambassador and board member.

00:14:54.580 --> 00:14:56.500
Hanging out with Oprah all the time

00:14:56.500 --> 00:15:01.170
is probably most people's wildest dream.

00:15:01.170 --> 00:15:04.900
But also, I would think, at
least this is me talking,

00:15:04.900 --> 00:15:06.440
I'd be a little intimidated.

00:15:06.440 --> 00:15:09.980
How do you manage a
relationship with Oprah

00:15:09.980 --> 00:15:13.390
and sort of get the best
that you can out of yourself

00:15:13.390 --> 00:15:15.530
and out of Oprah?

00:15:15.530 --> 00:15:18.810
One thing you have to know about Oprah,

00:15:18.810 --> 00:15:20.870
you have to know about Oprah,

00:15:20.870 --> 00:15:25.870
she is so diligent and specific

00:15:26.460 --> 00:15:30.680
about what she will do
and what she won't do.

00:15:30.680 --> 00:15:33.480
And it's the things that you say no to

00:15:34.360 --> 00:15:35.960
that actually really define you.

00:15:35.960 --> 00:15:40.203
So anything she does is purposeful.

00:15:41.060 --> 00:15:44.680
And what I realized is that we both saw

00:15:44.680 --> 00:15:47.720
the same opportunity for this brand

00:15:47.720 --> 00:15:52.130
that had actually been transforming
lives for so many years,

00:15:52.130 --> 00:15:57.113
but now in a new generation,
we could be so much more.

00:16:03.260 --> 00:16:04.830
As the world started to realize

00:16:04.830 --> 00:16:07.040
the extent of the COVID-19 pandemic,

00:16:07.040 --> 00:16:09.220
Grossman was on the road with Oprah,

00:16:09.220 --> 00:16:12.100
firing up arena-sized crowds
with their shared vision

00:16:12.100 --> 00:16:14.430
of a new kind of health and wellness,

00:16:14.430 --> 00:16:15.783
powered of course, by WW.

00:16:16.910 --> 00:16:18.080
Are there some days

00:16:19.460 --> 00:16:22.793
where you look at
yourself and you go, damn?

00:16:25.210 --> 00:16:28.080
Even COVID couldn't stop the momentum.

00:16:28.080 --> 00:16:29.220
With WW offices

00:16:29.220 --> 00:16:31.580
and its network of retail
locations shuttered

00:16:31.580 --> 00:16:33.390
as the world quickly went remote.

00:16:33.390 --> 00:16:35.080
Hey, everybody!

00:16:35.080 --> 00:16:36.620
Grossman's team created a four-week

00:16:36.620 --> 00:16:39.900
free virtual tour of
sorts starting in May.

00:16:39.900 --> 00:16:42.080
When we had to make that pivot

00:16:42.080 --> 00:16:44.112
when the pandemic happened--

00:16:44.112 --> 00:16:45.220
Yes!

00:16:45.220 --> 00:16:50.220
Within a few weeks we launched
a virtual tour led by Oprah

00:16:52.020 --> 00:16:57.020
and we made it available for
free to everyone globally.

00:16:58.330 --> 00:17:01.160
I see you all look very good up top,

00:17:01.160 --> 00:17:03.670
but no telling what you
got down on the bottom.

00:17:03.670 --> 00:17:06.190
And at some point,

00:17:06.190 --> 00:17:09.390
we all gonna have to
put real pants on again,

00:17:09.390 --> 00:17:11.430
and we're gonna hope they fit.

00:17:11.430 --> 00:17:13.060
That's what I'm doing, right?

00:17:13.060 --> 00:17:15.350
So speaking of board members, Mindy,

00:17:15.350 --> 00:17:17.100
one of the other people I
was able to catch up with

00:17:17.100 --> 00:17:19.130
was Julie Rice, who I know has become

00:17:19.130 --> 00:17:24.130
a friend of yours of late, but
that wasn't always the case.

00:17:24.130 --> 00:17:29.130
And so I actually asked her
how you guys met originally.

00:17:29.680 --> 00:17:31.940
So I want you to check this out.

00:17:31.940 --> 00:17:32.870
Julie is so great.

00:17:32.870 --> 00:17:34.593
I can't wait to, okay.

00:17:36.140 --> 00:17:39.490
Back when I was opening
my second SoulCycle

00:17:39.490 --> 00:17:42.840
on East 83rd Street, I
was a new entrepreneur

00:17:42.840 --> 00:17:45.520
and two mutual friends of Mindy's called

00:17:45.520 --> 00:17:46.730
and said that they were gonna be bringing

00:17:46.730 --> 00:17:48.790
Mindy Grossman to a class.

00:17:48.790 --> 00:17:51.020
Somebody like Mindy was
a full-blown celebrity.

00:17:51.020 --> 00:17:52.220
I couldn't think of a celebrity

00:17:52.220 --> 00:17:54.850
that I would rather have come
ride at SoulCycle than Mindy.

00:17:54.850 --> 00:17:57.160
I mean, I remember prepping
the front desk with flowers

00:17:57.160 --> 00:17:59.260
and making sure that I got
there early to make a sort of

00:17:59.260 --> 00:18:02.980
a perfect little swag
bag for her to take home.

00:18:02.980 --> 00:18:05.280
And I met her and she came
and she took the class.

00:18:05.280 --> 00:18:06.810
And the whole time she was in class

00:18:06.810 --> 00:18:09.520
I was just thinking to myself,
gosh, I hope she likes this.

00:18:09.520 --> 00:18:11.970
I just remember her being so tuned in

00:18:11.970 --> 00:18:14.130
to what we were trying to create there.

00:18:14.130 --> 00:18:16.440
And when a good girlfriend of mine

00:18:16.440 --> 00:18:18.220
sent me a text and she said,

00:18:18.220 --> 00:18:20.700
Mindy Grossman is going
to run WeightWatchers.

00:18:20.700 --> 00:18:23.690
That is something that you
should try to get in on.

00:18:23.690 --> 00:18:25.710
And so honestly, I stalked her.

00:18:25.710 --> 00:18:26.680
I sent her a note.

00:18:26.680 --> 00:18:27.860
We hadn't spoken in years.

00:18:27.860 --> 00:18:29.820
I had no idea if she would remember me,

00:18:29.820 --> 00:18:31.440
and sure enough she got back to me

00:18:31.440 --> 00:18:32.930
within the next hour or so.

00:18:32.930 --> 00:18:33.763
And she said, I'd love to.

00:18:33.763 --> 00:18:36.740
Do you want to have a cup of
coffee in the next week or so?

00:18:36.740 --> 00:18:38.150
That's sort of how our friendship began.

00:18:38.150 --> 00:18:40.200
And then ultimately I
wound up joining the board

00:18:40.200 --> 00:18:42.440
and I learn sitting next to her.

00:18:42.440 --> 00:18:44.600
I learn about being a better leader.

00:18:44.600 --> 00:18:48.070
I learn that the idea of not
innovating is scarier sometimes

00:18:48.070 --> 00:18:49.830
then shaking the whole thing up.

00:18:49.830 --> 00:18:52.110
I would just say that
Mindy is really brave

00:18:52.110 --> 00:18:53.843
and really innovative.

00:18:54.732 --> 00:18:55.573
Oh.

00:18:56.453 --> 00:18:58.220
Well, it sounds like she wasn't gonna stop

00:18:58.220 --> 00:19:00.963
until you guys were like, well-connected.

00:19:02.130 --> 00:19:03.660
I would not get in her way.

00:19:03.660 --> 00:19:06.320
The one thing she and I have in common

00:19:06.320 --> 00:19:09.950
is an insatiable sense of curiosity.

00:19:09.950 --> 00:19:14.740
And when we see something
or want to do something,

00:19:14.740 --> 00:19:16.830
we'll put ourselves out there.

00:19:16.830 --> 00:19:21.010
And I've kind of been
known to stalk people too,

00:19:21.010 --> 00:19:24.290
if I really want to learn more

00:19:24.290 --> 00:19:27.973
or be able to have a conversation.

00:19:28.850 --> 00:19:30.620
To go, how did you do that?

00:19:30.620 --> 00:19:32.660
Or why did you do that?

00:19:32.660 --> 00:19:34.720
If you're more curious,

00:19:34.720 --> 00:19:38.510
you're going to be more diverse
in your thinking in general.

00:19:38.510 --> 00:19:41.630
And so surrounding myself
with people like Julie

00:19:41.630 --> 00:19:44.810
and other members of our board

00:19:44.810 --> 00:19:48.820
is one of the most valuable
things that I, or we could do.

00:19:48.820 --> 00:19:53.150
You know, so Mindy, it's
interesting to interview,

00:19:53.150 --> 00:19:56.040
and I'll say this as like
a middle-aged white man

00:19:57.140 --> 00:19:58.750
asking you this.

00:19:58.750 --> 00:20:01.430
We strive so much to talk about,

00:20:01.430 --> 00:20:05.110
it's not a male CEO or a
female CEO, it's just a CEO.

00:20:05.110 --> 00:20:10.070
And yet you have been a
trailblazer by all accounts.

00:20:10.070 --> 00:20:12.340
I know a lot of women look up to you.

00:20:12.340 --> 00:20:17.340
So what advice are you giving
to women who are coming up?

00:20:18.180 --> 00:20:20.110
So I would say a couple of things.

00:20:20.110 --> 00:20:24.700
Number one is I believe the
reason I've been successful

00:20:24.700 --> 00:20:26.620
is I have focused my career

00:20:26.620 --> 00:20:29.870
on making other people successful.

00:20:29.870 --> 00:20:33.750
Because if I do that, they
will be more successful,

00:20:33.750 --> 00:20:35.530
the business will be more successful,

00:20:35.530 --> 00:20:38.930
and ultimately I will be more successful.

00:20:38.930 --> 00:20:42.230
The second thing is self-awareness

00:20:42.230 --> 00:20:44.820
is your greatest superpower.

00:20:44.820 --> 00:20:48.580
You need to truly understand your behavior

00:20:48.580 --> 00:20:50.600
and its impact on others.

00:20:50.600 --> 00:20:52.560
And know everything about yourself,

00:20:52.560 --> 00:20:54.930
whether it's the good,
the bad and the ugly.

00:20:54.930 --> 00:20:59.930
And know how you need to
show up to motivate people.

00:21:00.350 --> 00:21:04.150
The third thing is
progress, not perfection.

00:21:04.150 --> 00:21:06.840
I think too many women have felt

00:21:06.840 --> 00:21:09.020
that they don't put themselves out there

00:21:09.020 --> 00:21:11.580
because they have to wait to be perfect.

00:21:11.580 --> 00:21:16.350
And I think I make
people open up their eyes

00:21:16.350 --> 00:21:21.220
when I say I don't believe
I've truly been 100% ready

00:21:21.220 --> 00:21:24.490
for any job I've ever taken.

00:21:24.490 --> 00:21:29.380
But I've always defined
myself as a transformer

00:21:29.380 --> 00:21:33.860
with the ability to create
positive change around business,

00:21:33.860 --> 00:21:36.653
positive change around impact.

